# Things that must never leave this machine.
#
# Greg records what is said in a room in someone's home, remembers facts about
# them, and holds a working Spotify token. None of that is source code, and all
# of it is irreversible once pushed — a secret in a public repo is burned even
# if the next commit removes it.

# --- Secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
.env
spotify-tokens.json

# --- Personal data ---------------------------------------------------------
# The development log. It is the most useful document in the project and it is
# also a detailed portrait of one machine and one person's habits - the card and
# its memory budget, the monitor layout, and listening habits. It is addressed
# to whoever is working on Greg
# next, not to a reader, and several of its sections are forward-looking notes
# that would be stale the day they were published.
#
# Everything in it that CONSTRAINS the code - the settings that look like
# improvements and are not - is extracted into DECISIONS.md, which does ship.
# Source comments saying "see CLAUDE.md" refer to this file; DECISIONS.md says
# so, so the reference is explained rather than dangling.
CLAUDE.md
# config.json carries your location and coordinates. config.example.json is the
# one that ships; copy it to config.json on a new machine.
config.json
# A verbatim log of everything said in front of him, across restarts.
conversations.jsonl
# Facts he has been told to remember about you.
memory.json
# Your timers, reminders and what they are for.
reminders.json
# What your dials have become, as opposed to where they started.
personality.json
# Pictures of your screen.
screenshots/

# --- Someone else's voice --------------------------------------------------
# The cloned voice is built from a real person's recording. That is their voice,
# not this project's, and it is not ours to publish. Piper's downloaded voice
# models are large binaries that belong to their own projects too.
voices/
# And the SAME VOICE lives here too, which the rule above does not cover.
# voice-audition/ is a listening comparison built during development. It holds
# the reference recording plus five clones made from it, which are synthetic
# speech in that person's voice. The clones are the more sensitive half: a
# published voice clone of an identifiable person is the material voice-cloning
# abuse is made of, and permission to use a recording for one private purpose is
# not permission to publish it to anyone who wants it.
#
# Ignored WHOLESALE and staying that way. Parts of the folder are Piper output
# from public models and would be safe on their own, but un-ignoring it means
# hand-deleting individual files, and missing one publishes a voice clone of a
# real person - irreversibly, since a repository can be cloned before a later
# commit removes anything. The durable finding from that comparison is written
# up in DECISIONS.md instead, which costs nothing and risks nothing.
voice-audition/

# --- Build, cache and downloads --------------------------------------------
node_modules/
cache/
.venv/
.venv-clone/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- Editor and OS ---------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
